A Streamlit-based tracker for U.S. Congress stock transactions. It monitors publicly disclosed House and Senate trades, tracks selected politicians and their transactions, and provides alerts when new trades are reported.

Data pipeline
-------------
fetch_trades.py runs daily (see .github/workflows/fetch-trades.yml) and pulls
from a free, keyless, no-rate-limit feed (kadoa-org/congress-trading-monitor's
public/data/trades.json, sourced from official House Clerk / Senate eFD /
OGE filings). It writes the merged dataset to data/trades.json, which
index.html / app.js reads directly. Search always runs over the full
dataset; the table just paginates the matches 50 at a time so it stays
fast to render. The "Top 5 Whale Trades" dashboard highlights the largest
disclosed trades (by dollar amount) from the last 30 days.

Email alerts
------------
Visitors manage their own subscription from the "Email Alerts" tab, which has
two modes:

Subscribe: pick one or more politicians, enter an email, and "Continue on
GitHub" opens a pre-filled issue titled "Subscribe request: <email>". This
does NOT subscribe them yet - process_subscription.py (run by
.github/workflows/process-subscriptions.yml on every new issue matching one
of the three title prefixes below) validates the request (real-looking
email, every politician must match a filer name already in data/trades.json),
records it in pending_subscriptions.json with a random token, emails a
confirmation link to that address, comments on the issue explaining to check
their inbox, and closes it. The link opens a second pre-filled issue titled
"Confirm subscription: <token>"; submitting *that* is what actually moves
the entry into alerts.json. Tokens expire after 48 hours and are single-use.
This means a subscription can only take effect if whoever opened the first
issue also controls the inbox it named.

Unsubscribe: same tab, same politician picker (or an "unsubscribe from all"
checkbox), opens a "Unsubscribe request: <email>" issue. This is processed
immediately, no confirmation email - anyone who knows an address can turn
its alerts off, which is low-harm (worst case someone has to resubscribe).

Invalid or expired requests get a rejection comment explaining why and are
left open. A submitter needs a free GitHub account to open any of these
issues; anyone can technically open one against the repo, so treat
alerts.json / pending_subscriptions.json as low-trust data (a bogus entry
is just a JSON line to revert). Note that alerts.json is a plain file in a
public repo, so subscriber emails are publicly visible - fine for a small
group of people who know that going in, not something to point at a large
audience without changing that.

alerts.json can also be edited by hand, same format:

   [
     { "member": "Nancy Pelosi", "email": "you@example.com" }
   ]

   "member" must match the filer name exactly as it appears in
   data/trades.json (case-insensitive).

To actually send the mail, add these repository secrets (Settings ->
Secrets and variables -> Actions) so fetch-trades.yml's alert step can
reach an SMTP server:

   - SMTP_HOST     e.g. smtp.gmail.com
   - SMTP_PORT     e.g. 587 (optional, defaults to 587)
   - SMTP_USERNAME your SMTP account / email address
   - SMTP_PASSWORD an app password (Gmail: Google Account -> Security ->
                    2-Step Verification -> App passwords). Don't use your
                    normal account password.
   - SMTP_FROM     optional, defaults to SMTP_USERNAME

Without those secrets set, send_alerts.py simply skips sending mail (the
daily workflow still runs and updates trades.json normally).
